      "lastReply": "A young tiger named Raju lived deep in the forest, where he spent his days hunting and napping under banyan trees. One evening, the wind carried the smell of biryani from a village nearby, and Raju followed it to a wedding feast. He watched from the shadows as the cook layered rice, spices and meat in a huge pot, and something stirred in him—he wanted to make that magic himself.\n\nThe next morning, Raju crept into the village and found the cook's recipe book lying on a bench. He couldn't read well, but he memorised the pictures: golden onions, bright red chillies, fragrant cardamom. Back in the forest, he gathered what he could—wild herbs, a clay pot he borrowed from a confused monkey, and a chicken who owed him a favour. His first attempt was a disaster: the rice burned, the spices clashed, and the chicken complained the whole time.\n\nBut Raju didn't give up. He practised every week, adjusting the heat, balancing the masala, learning patience as the pot simmered. One day, the forest animals gathered around his fire, drawn by the smell, and when he lifted the lid, steam rose like a cloud of stars. They ate together under the moon, and Raju realised he'd found something better than any hunt—a way to bring everyone to the table. From then on, he was known not as the fiercest tiger, but as the one who made the best biryani in the jungle.\n\n4:41 AM",
